   ("p", "Morse code represents text as sequences of two symbols: the dot (a short signal) and the dash (a long one). Samuel Morse and Alfred Vail developed it in the 1830s and 1840s so that messages could travel down a telegraph wire as simple on-off pulses. Because it needs only a single channel that can be switched on and off, Morse can be sent as sound, light, a tapped finger or an electrical pulse, which is exactly why it has survived so long: any medium that can be turned on and off can carry it."),
